**Two Sum** asks for two distinct indices in an array whose values add up to a target.

**Optimal idea:** scan once while storing each number’s index in a hash map. For each `nums[i]`, check whether `target - nums[i]` was seen before; if yes, return those two indices. This is `O(n)` time and `O(n)` space.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            int need = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.TryGetValue(need, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            if (!seen.ContainsKey(nums[i]))
                seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }

        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** `O(n)` time, `O(n)` space.
